is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: router_core_1
diagnosis_text:
The failed component is the OSPF adjacency over the direct point-to-point link between router_core_1:eth1 (172.16.0.2/31) and router_core_3:eth0 (172.16.0.3/31) in area 0.0.0.0. Mechanism: the physical/data-link on router_core_1’s eth1 is down, so FRR no longer runs OSPF on that interface and the neighbor to router_core_3 drops. Traffic remains unaffected because routes are reconverging via router_core_2, preserving reachability to the server farm.

Localization evidence:
- On router_core_1, eth1 is administratively/physically down: ip addr shows state DOWN and ifconfig lacks UP/RUNNING, and ethtool reports “Link detected: no”. FRR shows no OSPF neighbor on eth1 and “show ip ospf interface” lists only eth0 and eth2.
- On router_core_3, the peer interface eth0 is UP with “Link detected: yes”, and OSPF still shows the neighbor 172.16.0.0 on eth0 (dead timer counting down). 
- ICMP between the routers succeeds via the alternate path (R1↔R2↔R3), and router_core_1’s routing table even learns 172.16.0.2/31 via 172.16.0.1 (R2), confirming the direct link is not acting as a connected network.

Root cause: the router_core_1 eth1-to-router_core_3 eth0 backbone link is down on the router_core_1 side (cable/port failure or interface left administratively down).

Corrective fix: restore the physical link or bring the interface up on router_core_1 (verify cabling/veth, then ip link set dev eth1 up). After link comes up, OSPF on eth1 should reestablish to router_core_3, restoring path redundancy. === END ===